{"status": "ok", "message-type": "work", "message-version": "1.0.0", "message": {"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2026, 10, 23]], "date-time": "2026-10-23T21:17:06Z", "timestamp": 1792790226894, "version": "4.5.0"}, "reference-count": 123, "publisher": "American Astronomical Society", "issue": "4", "license": [{"start": {"date-parts": [[2026, 9, 29]], "date-time": "2026-09-29T00:00:00Z", "timestamp": 1790640000000}, "content-version": "vor", "delay-in-days": 0, "URL": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"}, {"start": {"date-parts": [[2026, 9, 29]], "date-time": "2026-09-29T00:00:00Z", "timestamp": 1790640000000}, "content-version": "tdm", "delay-in-days": 0, "URL": "https://iopscience.iop.org/info/page/text-and-data-mining"}], "funder": [{"name": "Space Telescope Science Institute", "award": ["NAS5-03127"], "award-info": [{"award-number": ["NAS5-03127"]}], "id": [{"id": "https://ror.org/036f5mx38", "id-type": "ROR", "asserted-by": "crossref"}]}, {"name": "Canadian Space Agency", "award": ["23JWGO2B06"], "award-info": [{"award-number": ["23JWGO2B06"]}], "id": [{"id": "https://ror.org/03a1gte98", "id-type": "ROR", "asserted-by": "crossref"}]}], "content-domain": {"domain": ["iopscience.iop.org"], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "short-container-title": ["AJ"], "published-print": {"date-parts": [[2026, 10, 1]]}, "abstract": "<jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>\n                    Ultrashort period (USP) rocky planets are expected to be depleted of any substantial gaseous envelope due to the intense irradiation they receive from their host star, making the recent detection of an atmosphere around TOI-561 b particularly surprising. That finding was based on the planet’s bulk density and dayside emission spectrum, but full-orbit phase-curve observations offer a more comprehensive way to constrain the presence and characteristics of a planet’s atmosphere. In this paper, we map TOI-561 b’s 3–5 \n                    <jats:italic>μ</jats:italic>\n                    m emission using JWST/NIRSpec multiorbit spectroscopic phase curves, explicitly accounting for the curvature of the out-of-eclipse baseline and possible hotspot offsets. From a simple energy balance argument as well as comparing to general circulation models, we find the phase-curve observations are best explained with high Bond albedo and moderate global heat transfer. Our 37 hr continuous observation provides a long baseline that allows us to model stellar granulation, the planetary phase curve, and dayside emission simultaneously, effectively disentangling these signals and yielding a dayside emission spectrum that is more robust against stellar variability and consistent with previous eclipse-only fits. Using general circulation model outputs, we constrain where clouds can plausibly form and test candidate compositions, finding that silicate clouds, such as SiO\n                    <jats:sub>2</jats:sub>\n                    and MgSiO\n                    <jats:sub>3</jats:sub>\n                    , can form on the dayside near the terminator and explain the observed albedo. Our findings confirm that TOI-561 b appears to have a global reflective atmosphere, suggesting exchange of volatiles with the interior to maintain it.\n                  </jats:p>", "DOI": "10.3847/1538-3881/ae9763", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2026, 9, 29]], "date-time": "2026-09-29T06:51:27Z", "timestamp": 1790664687000}, "page": "239", "update-policy": "https://doi.org/10.1088/crossmark-policy", "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["A Highly Reflective Atmosphere on the Lava World TOI-561b Revealed by JWST/NIRSpec Phase Curves"], "prefix": "10.3847", "volume": "172", "author": [{"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0009-0003-5977-9581", "authenticated-orcid": true, "given": "Samuel", "family": "Boucher", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [{"name": "Université de Montréal"}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4987-6591", "authenticated-orcid": true, "given": "Lisa", "family": "Dang", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [{"name": "Université de Montréal"}, {"name": "University of Waterloo; Waterloo"}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0009-0000-8327-2631", "authenticated-orcid": false, "given": "Alex", "family": "McGinty", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [{"name": "University of Oxford"}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0009-0008-2801-5040", "authenticated-orcid": true, "given": "Johanna K.", "family": "Teske", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [{"name": "Carnegie Institution for Science"}, {"name": "The Observatories of the Carnegie Institution for Science"}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5887-1197", "authenticated-orcid": true, "given": "Raymond", "family": "Pierrehumbert", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [{"name": "University of Oxford"}, {"name": "Massachusetts Institute of Technology"}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4487-5533", "authenticated-orcid": true, "given": "Anjali A. A.", "family": "Piette", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [{"name": "University of Birmingham"}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0354-0187", "authenticated-orcid": true, "given": "Nicole L.", "family": "Wallack", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [{"name": "Carnegie Institution for Science"}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5341-8978", "authenticated-orcid": false, "given": "Angharad", "family": "Weeks", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [{"name": "University of Sydney"}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"